::SPEECH_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan best shows Unit 2 synthesis?
{
~A. A topic and three unrelated facts.#This lacks focus, organization, support, transitions, and format.
=B. A focus, logical points, support, reasoning, transitions, conclusion, and speech-ready notes.#Correct. This combines the major Unit 2 informative speech skills.
~C. A copied paragraph from a source.#This does not show paraphrase or speech-ready planning.
~D. A list of random speaking words.#Random words do not create an informative speech plan.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Organized note cards help speakers remember the order of their ideas. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Note cards make ideas unnecessary.#This changes the source meaning.
~B. Speakers should copy every word on note cards.#This adds an inaccurate idea.
=C. Note cards arranged clearly can help speakers keep their ideas in order.#Correct. This restates the source accurately in new wording.
~D. Organized notes do not affect speaking.#This contradicts the source meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which structure best explains steps for preparing a speech?
{
=A. Chronological order#Correct. Steps are easiest to understand in time order.
~B. Random order#Random order would confuse the process.
~C. Unrelated details#Unrelated details do not explain steps.
~D. Hidden structure#A hidden structure does not guide the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech product is most presentation-ready?
{
~A. A full source paragraph to read.#This may cause reading instead of presenting.
~B. A title with no points.#This is incomplete because a presentation-ready plan needs organized points and support for the speaker to follow.
~C. A list of vague ideas.#Vague ideas do not guide delivery.
=D. Short notes with focus, points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. This format supports clear presentation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support best fits the point "Rehearsal improves timing"?
{
~A. Some rooms have clocks.#This is related to time but not rehearsal.
=B. A speaker can time each section during practice and shorten parts that run long.#Correct. This support directly explains the point.
~C. Timing is a word used in speeches.#This does not support the point.
~D. Some students enjoy speeches.#This is unrelated to timing.
}
